[36m(main_task pid=2489016)[0m Doc 1(Title: "Calamus caryotoides") Calamus caryotoides Calamus caryotoides (also ""Palmijuncus caryotoides""), more commonly known as fishtail lawyer cane is a North-East Queensland tropical forest climbing palm with very thin () flexible trunks; no crownshaft; small spikes; dark green, glossy, fish-tail shaped leaves reaching up to high ( spread); and very thin hooked flagella. It tends to clump and grow up into the shaded understory of Queensland's wet tropical forests, and is a close relative of the more infamous ""Calamus radicalis"" (aka Wait-a-While). The Cairns Botanical Gardens records local Yidinydji, Yirrganyydji, Djabuganydji, and Gungganydji use ""Calamus caryotoides"" (also known to Yidinydji as Bugul, pronounced BOOK-KOOL)
